# Review is a hard gate

Any change that affects what the user sees or how the app behaves is not done
when the code compiles and tests pass. It is done only after a full two-pass
review against the test matrix below. Never ask the user to be your first
reviewer.

This applies to UI, UX, store/cache logic, shared contracts, database mappings,
server/API wiring, shortcuts, modals, and any other change where the user would
notice stale data, wrong state, leaked data, or a crash. Unit tests alone are
not enough.

## Two-pass gate

For large features, integrations, and anything touching Team or manager flows:

1. Build the complete matrix before opening the browser.
2. Run every row in the matrix once.
3. Fix every product, UI, functional, permission, and data-leak bug found.
4. Run every failed row again.
5. Run neighboring flows touched by the fixes again.
6. Run a second pass over the affected matrix before calling the work done.

The report must name what was tested, what failed, what was fixed, what was
retested, and any state that could not be reached.

## Step zero: Playwright auth

1. Every user-visible change ships with Playwright specs in `apps/e2e/tests/`.
2. E2E resets the Neon `E2E_DATABASE_URL` database, runs `pnpm db:migrate` and
   `pnpm db:seed`, then `auth.setup.ts` signs in as `testuser@egain.com` /
   `egain@123` (and manager), saving storage state for specs. See `apps/e2e/README.md`.
3. Run `pnpm test:e2e` before calling work done. Attach pass output to the
   review report.
4. Add or extend a spec for every matrix row. No row without a spec.

If any flow in the matrix cannot be exercised, the work is not done. Fix access
or stop and say so. An auth wall is never an excuse.

Devtools MCP (`chrome-devtools`) is optional debug-only, not a ship gate.

## Build the test matrix first

Before touching the browser, enumerate every entry point, every control, and
every value of every control affected by the change. Write it down, then test
each row.

Rules:

- Every segment of a segmented control gets tested, not just the default.
- Every toggle gets tested on both sides.
- Every list gets tested empty and overflowing.
- Every keyboard shortcut gets tested from every screen state where it applies.
- Every modal gets tested with enough content to overflow the viewport.
- Every global handler (`window` listeners, shortcuts, portals) gets a
  single-owner audit: grep for other registrations of the same key or event,
  and confirm only one is live per screen state.

## Playwright spec rules

1. One spec file per feature area (`apps/e2e/jira/`, `apps/e2e/projects/`, etc.).
2. Tag Jira-connected flows with `@jira`. CI excludes them via `grepInvert`; specs
   also skip when `/jira/status` reports not connected.
3. Use desktop, tablet, and phone projects from `playwright.config.ts` when the
   change touches pane layout.
4. Assert observable DOM state, not implementation details.
5. Capture `screenshot` on failure for visual regressions.

## Per-flow steps (encode in Playwright)

1. Navigate to the screen that owns the changed behavior.
2. Reach the exact state you changed. Select the task, switch tabs, drag a
   row, submit a form, open the modal from every entry point.
3. Assert structure and pixels where UI changed.
4. Exercise the interaction end to end. Confirm the observable result, not just
   that nothing threw.
5. Console errors during the spec are a defect of this change until proven
   otherwise. Listen with `page.on("console")` when debugging.

## Functional checks every time

- Cache and refetch: after a mutation, does the UI update without a full page
  reload?
- Integration edges: success, auth failure, unavailable dependency, malformed
  remote data, empty remote data, stale cached data, retry, and remote updates
  arriving while the UI is open.
- React hooks: no hooks after early returns or inside conditionals.
- Cross-tab state: switching tabs or selecting another task must not crash or
  leave stale panels visible.
- Optimistic updates: on failure, does the UI roll back cleanly?
- Shortcuts: test with focus in an input, with a dialog open, and with focus
  on the page body.

## UI checks every time

- shadcn-first: do not hand-roll controls when a shadcn or Radix equivalent
  exists (Select, Dialog, Toggle, fieldset). Custom composition only for
  domain-specific UI.
- Visual fidelity: for polish work, read before/after screenshots. Fail on
  blocky toolbars, misaligned chevrons, and controls that look like inputs but
  behave like buttons.
- Neighboring states: selected and unselected, empty and populated, completed
  and active, dragging and idle.
- Every bucket and route that renders the component, not only the one you were
  looking at. If the change touches a shared component, the matrix must name
  every route that imports it. Today and Unscheduled both render TaskRow and
  NewTaskDialog. Inbox, Week, and first-run count if they share the same
  component.
- A named route is Pass only if you navigated to that exact URL and the
  snapshot URL matches. No snapshot of `/unscheduled` means Unscheduled was
  not reviewed. Claiming it was is a failed review.
- A crash overlay is an automatic fail: "Something went wrong",
  "is not defined", a React error boundary, or a blank main with a console
  exception. Open every named route before declaring the work done.
- Alignment and baseline of siblings.
- Keyboard path and focus order, plus `aria-label` on any icon-only control.
- Motion: run the transition, then confirm no leftover inline `transform` or
  `transition` sticks to the node afterwards.

## Viewport matrix for pane features

Any change to Today, report day, report inbox, Jira, or inspector panes must
be reviewed at three widths:

- Phone: below 768px
- Tablet: 768px to 1279px
- Desktop: 1280px and up

A desktop-only screenshot does not cover tablet. Tablet uses the same three
panes as desktop (tasks, Jira, inspector). Phone keeps the task list and opens
Jira in a sheet from a task-list header control, not a sixth bottom-nav item.

## Jira list versus preview

Clicking a Jira issue must keep the Jira list mounted and open preview in the
inspector (or the phone Jira sheet). Replacing the middle pane with issue
detail is a fail.

## Manager report Jira

Sign in as a manager, open `/team/$userId`, and confirm assigned issues **or**
a specific empty reason such as "this report has no matching Jira user". A
generic "Could not load Jira issues" error is a fail unless the request itself
failed and Retry is offered.

## Mutation buttons

Explicit mutation buttons (connect, save, delete, send, invite, create, link,
comment) must show in-flight loading, refuse a second submit, and toast
success. Autosave, drag reorder, and notification mark-read stay quiet.

Mutation forms must disable their inputs and editors while busy. A screenshot
mid-submit that shows editable fields is an automatic fail.

In-flight is a required screenshot, not a unit-test proxy. Capture the frame
after click, before the toast. Fail if the label doubles, stays fully visible
beside the spinner, the control grows or shrinks, or the spinner walks off
center. Claiming loaders work from `aria-busy` alone is a fail.

Primary titles must wrap. A single-line input that clips a heading is a fail.
Truncate only meta. Title weight in a side list must not exceed the neighboring
task list.

Jira row Create task must open the create dialog with the issue title filled.
Opening preview instead is a fail.

A refetch must not cover, dim, or block the list or inspector. The existing
refresh icon spinning is enough. A frost overlay on an already-visible list is
a fail.

Rich text verification: toolbar actions must survive save and re-open (bold,
list, link, table with chosen size). Jira column matrix must cover pagination
load-more, preset select, custom JQL dialog, compact components trigger, and
status color checks for Resolved, Fix Verified, and Closed on the same list.

## Team and manager flows

When Team, manager, report, Inbox, or Jira behavior changes, the matrix must
cover personal view, manager roster, report day, report week, report inbox,
send-to-inbox, accept, decline, reorder, delete where allowed, comments where
allowed, private task hiding, public project assignment, Jira issue visibility,
Jira task linking, timeline side effects, notification side effects, and no
leak to uninvolved users.

## Own the feature, not the diff

The matrix above tests what changed. This section tests what the feature
promises. A thorough diff review that misses an obvious gap in the same feature
is still a failed review.

- **Full verb sweep.** For every list of things the UI renders, check create,
  read, update, delete, reorder, and toggle. Name any verb the user cannot
  perform. A missing verb is a defect of the feature even when it predates
  your change.
- **Dead plumbing check.** Grep for exported mutations and API routes touching
  the feature. Anything with no caller is a gap: report it, and wire it if it
  is small.
- **Dead controls.** Every button, checkbox, and input in the changed area must
  do something when clicked. A control with no handler, or a handler that
  receives `undefined`, is a defect.
- **Both branches.** When a component renders one of two versions of a list,
  test both. Confirm the one users actually hit is the one that got the fix.
- **Fix or log.** Small adjacent defects get fixed in the same change. Larger
  ones get named explicitly in the report. Silence is not an option.

Add "out of scope" to the banned-phrase list when it is used to skip a defect
found inside the feature under review.

## Report format

State results as a table:

| Matrix row | Playwright spec | Result |

Banned phrases without a passing `pnpm test:e2e` run:

- "should work"
- "verified by tests"
- "looks correct"
- "both routes" / "Today and Unscheduled" unless each URL has its own spec

If Playwright cannot reach a state, say so plainly and name the untested state.
Never claim a change is verified from unit tests alone.

## Platforms

Run the matrix on web (`http://localhost:5173`). When the change touches
desktop-specific behavior (Tauri shell, native shortcuts), also run on desktop
(`pnpm dev:desktop`).
